insert into public.project_contacts (company, company_id, contact_name, title, linkedin_url, email, priority, target_type_raw, outreach_angle, value_hypothesis, stakeholder_selection_reason, linkedin_connect_message, intro_message, follow_up_message, email_subject, email_intro_message, email_follow_up_1, email_follow_up_2, email_assumption_notice) values ($$Interplay Learning$$, '0e7f66fc-334c-477d-814c-8b4219ee5777', $$John Pumpelly$$, $$Chief Financial Officer$$, $$https://www.linkedin.com/in/pumpelly$$, $$jpumpelly@interplaylearning.com$$, $$A$$, $$Target 1$$, $$Interplay Learning is hiring its first dedicated Senior Director of Revenue Operations, reporting to the CFO, to create a single source of truth across Sales, Marketing, Customer Success, Finance, forecasting, Salesforce, systems governance, and the full revenue lifecycle. John is the explicit reporting manager and executive owner of the build.$$, $$Chad and Harsh can help John accelerate the commercial architecture around the internal hire by defining lifecycle stages, source-of-truth metrics, forecast logic, systems governance, executive dashboards, and an implementation sequence that connects Interplay's software, training services, customer lifecycle, and recent ITI integration.$$, $$Explicit reporting manager and primary functional owner. The current Senior Director of Revenue Operations posting states that the role reports to the CFO, and Interplay Learning identifies John Pumpelly as Chief Financial Officer.$$, $$Hi John, Interplay's first dedicated Senior Director of RevOps search caught my attention. The mandate to create one source of truth across Sales, Marketing, Customer Success, Finance, and forecasting closely matches systems I have built. I would value connecting.$$, $$Thanks for connecting, John. Based on your work as Chief Financial Officer at Interplay Learning, I wanted to be direct about why I reached out. RevHub helps organizations connect customer and market intelligence, segmentation, pricing, marketing, sales execution, operations, forecasting and executive reporting into one commercial growth system. For Interplay Learning, the opportunity I see is: Chad and Harsh can help John accelerate the commercial architecture around the internal hire by defining lifecycle stages, source-of-truth metrics, forecast logic, systems governance, executive dashboards, and an implementation sequence that connects Interplay's software, training services, customer lifecycle, and recent ITI integration. Would you be open to a quick 15-minute call to discuss what we do and whether it could support your priorities?$$, $$Hi John, following up in case my earlier note got buried. I outlined the commercial growth approach behind RevHub here: https://aboutchad.com/approach. Based on your role at Interplay Learning, I believe there may be a relevant opportunity around interplay learning is hiring its first dedicated senior director of revenue operations, reporting to the cfo, to create a single source of truth across sales, marketing, customer success, finance, forecasting, salesforce, systems governance, and the full revenue lifecycle. john is the explicit reporting manager and executive owner of the build. Would you be open to a quick 15-minute call to discuss the approach and whether it could be useful to your team?$$, $$A faster path to Interplay's revenue source of truth$$, $$Hi John,

I saw Interplay Learning's search for its first dedicated Senior Director of Revenue Operations. The mandate is substantial: create one source of truth across Sales, Marketing, Customer Success, and Finance, strengthen forecasting, govern Salesforce and the broader GTM stack, and establish the operating cadence behind the full revenue lifecycle.

That work is closely aligned with the commercial systems I have spent more than 15 years building. At QXO, I helped connect fragmented CRM, marketing automation, customer, credit, call-center, and operational data while scaling digital revenue from $400M to $1.25B. Through RevHub, my partner Harsh and I combine commercial architecture, data strategy, forecasting, analytics, and hands-on implementation.

For Interplay, a focused first phase could establish the lifecycle definitions, source-of-truth metrics, forecast inputs, systems ownership, dashboard requirements, and decision rules the new RevOps leader should inherit. That would reduce discovery time and create earlier value across the software, training-services, and customer lifecycle.

Would you be open to a 20-minute conversation next week? Our approach is here: https://www.aboutchad.com/approach

Best,
Chad Parker$$, $$Hi John,

One practical deliverable would be a revenue architecture connecting acquisition, qualification, pipeline, delivery, product adoption, customer health, retention, expansion, and financial planning. It would identify where Interplay's current visibility is limited by data, definitions, process, or ownership and give the internal hire a sequenced roadmap.

Would a brief conversation next week be worthwhile?$$, $$Hi John,

Closing the loop. The opportunity I see is helping Interplay establish the commercial operating model before the new RevOps leader has to discover and reconcile every dependency independently.

Is there a 20-minute window worth holding, or is another executive closer to the initial architecture work?$$, $$Assumed from the first-initial-plus-last-name pattern demonstrated by the verified public address ddonovan@interplaylearning.com. This email may not work and should be verified before sending.$$);

insert into public.project_contacts (company, company_id, contact_name, title, linkedin_url, email, priority, target_type_raw, outreach_angle, value_hypothesis, stakeholder_selection_reason, linkedin_connect_message, intro_message, follow_up_message, email_subject, email_intro_message, email_follow_up_1, email_follow_up_2, email_assumption_notice) values ($$Interplay Learning$$, '0e7f66fc-334c-477d-814c-8b4219ee5777', $$Doug Donovan$$, $$Chief Executive Officer and Founder$$, $$https://www.linkedin.com/in/doug-donovan-141b842b$$, $$ddonovan@interplaylearning.com$$, $$A$$, $$Target 2$$, $$Interplay is scaling an AI- and simulation-enabled skilled-trades training platform while integrating Industrial Training International and building its first dedicated RevOps leadership function. Doug is the founder-CEO and executive sponsor accountable for growth, portfolio integration, customer value, and company-wide decision quality.$$, $$Chad and Harsh can help Doug connect Interplay's market segments, software and training portfolio, acquisition, pipeline, implementation, learner engagement, retention, expansion, and financial outcomes into one executive growth model while the internal RevOps team builds the production systems.$$, $$Founder-CEO and executive sponsor above the Senior Director mandate. Interplay Learning's current leadership materials identify Doug Donovan as CEO and Founder, accountable for the company's growth, platform strategy, customer impact, and integration of Industrial Training International.$$, $$Hi Doug, Interplay's current RevOps build and continued expansion across immersive skilled-trades training caught my attention. Connecting portfolio growth, customer lifecycle data, forecasting, and executive decisions closely matches transformations I have led. I would value connecting.$$, $$Thanks for connecting, Doug. Based on your work as Chief Executive Officer and Founder at Interplay Learning, I wanted to be direct about why I reached out. RevHub helps organizations connect customer and market intelligence, segmentation, pricing, marketing, sales execution, operations, forecasting and executive reporting into one commercial growth system. For Interplay Learning, the opportunity I see is: Chad and Harsh can help Doug connect Interplay's market segments, software and training portfolio, acquisition, pipeline, implementation, learner engagement, retention, expansion, and financial outcomes into one executive growth model while the internal RevOps team builds the production systems. Would you be open to a quick 15-minute call to discuss what we do and whether it could support your priorities?$$, $$Hi Doug, following up in case my earlier note got buried. I outlined the commercial growth approach behind RevHub here: https://aboutchad.com/approach. Based on your role at Interplay Learning, I believe there may be a relevant opportunity around interplay is scaling an ai- and simulation-enabled skilled-trades training platform while integrating industrial training international and building its first dedicated revops leadership function. doug is the founder-ceo and executive sponsor accountable for growth, portfolio integration, customer value, and company-wide decision quality. Would you be open to a quick 15-minute call to discuss the approach and whether it could be useful to your team?$$, $$Connecting Interplay's growth engine after ITI$$, $$Hi Doug,

Interplay's expansion across immersive skilled-trades training, AI-enabled learning, and the Industrial Training International portfolio creates a meaningful growth opportunity, but also a more complex commercial operating model. The current search for Interplay's first dedicated Senior Director of Revenue Operations suggests the company is ready to connect that growth through one view of customers, pipeline, delivery, engagement, retention, expansion, and forecasting.

I have spent more than 15 years building those systems. At QXO, I helped scale digital revenue from $400M to $1.25B while connecting fragmented CRM, marketing, customer, credit, call-center, and operational data. Through RevHub, my partner Harsh and I combine commercial strategy, data architecture, forecasting, analytics, and implementation capacity.

For Interplay, an initial engagement could define the executive decision model behind the RevOps build: the customer and portfolio segments that matter most, the lifecycle and source-of-truth measures beneath them, and the operating cadence needed to turn insight into action. That would help the internal leader create value earlier while reducing the burden of discovering every dependency from scratch.

Would you be open to a 20-minute conversation next week? Our approach is here: https://www.aboutchad.com/approach

Best,
Chad Parker$$, $$Hi Doug,

One concrete deliverable could be an executive growth map connecting Interplay's market segments and product portfolio to acquisition, sales conversion, implementation, learner engagement, customer value, retention, expansion, and contribution economics. It would help leadership see where the ITI integration and broader platform are creating leverage and where operating constraints need attention.

Would a brief conversation next week be useful?$$, $$Hi Doug,

Closing the loop. Interplay already has differentiated technology, content, and customer impact. The opportunity I see is building the commercial decision system that helps leadership scale those assets through the same evidence.

Is there a 20-minute window worth holding?$$, $$Verified public business email published by Doug Donovan in a public LinkedIn reply.$$);

insert into public.project_contacts (company, company_id, contact_name, title, linkedin_url, email, priority, target_type_raw, outreach_angle, value_hypothesis, stakeholder_selection_reason, linkedin_connect_message, intro_message, follow_up_message, email_subject, email_intro_message, email_follow_up_1, email_follow_up_2, email_assumption_notice) values ($$Interplay Learning$$, '0e7f66fc-334c-477d-814c-8b4219ee5777', $$Emma Vas$$, $$Vice President of Marketing$$, $$https://www.linkedin.com/in/emmavas$$, $$evas@interplaylearning.com$$, $$A$$, $$Target 3$$, $$Interplay's first dedicated RevOps leader will define lifecycle reporting, marketing-to-sales handoffs, source data, attribution, pipeline visibility, and executive measurement. Emma leads Marketing and is the senior functional collaborator most affected by whether demand activity becomes trusted revenue insight.$$, $$Chad can help Emma connect market and customer segmentation, campaign taxonomy, CRM stages, attribution, pipeline, product and learner engagement, retention, and executive reporting into one growth model while the new RevOps function establishes the underlying governance.$$, $$VP-level functional collaborator above the Senior Director signal. Emma Vas is Interplay Learning's Vice President of Marketing and is directly affected by the role's ownership of marketing operations, lifecycle reporting, attribution, CRM governance, pipeline visibility, and executive measurement.$$, $$Hi Emma, Interplay's current RevOps build caught my attention. Your marketing mandate makes the connection between audience strategy, campaign data, pipeline, customer engagement, and trusted revenue measurement especially relevant. I would value connecting.$$, $$Thanks for connecting, Emma. Based on your work as Vice President of Marketing at Interplay Learning, I wanted to be direct about why I reached out. RevHub helps organizations connect customer and market intelligence, segmentation, pricing, marketing, sales execution, operations, forecasting and executive reporting into one commercial growth system. For Interplay Learning, the opportunity I see is: Chad can help Emma connect market and customer segmentation, campaign taxonomy, CRM stages, attribution, pipeline, product and learner engagement, retention, and executive reporting into one growth model while the new RevOps function establishes the underlying governance. Would you be open to a quick 15-minute call to discuss what we do and whether it could support your priorities?$$, $$Hi Emma, following up in case my earlier note got buried. I outlined the commercial growth approach behind RevHub here: https://aboutchad.com/approach. Based on your role at Interplay Learning, I believe there may be a relevant opportunity around interplay's first dedicated revops leader will define lifecycle reporting, marketing-to-sales handoffs, source data, attribution, pipeline visibility, and executive measurement. emma leads marketing and is the senior functional collaborator most affected by whether demand activity becomes trusted revenue insight. Would you be open to a quick 15-minute call to discuss the approach and whether it could be useful to your team?$$, $$From marketing activity to Interplay revenue visibility$$, $$Hi Emma,

Interplay's search for its first dedicated Senior Director of Revenue Operations caught my attention because the value of that role will depend heavily on how Marketing and RevOps define the customer lifecycle together. The challenge is not simply better attribution. It is connecting audience strategy, campaigns, CRM stages, pipeline, implementation, learner engagement, retention, and expansion through one trusted model.

I have spent more than 15 years building that connective layer. At QXO, I helped scale digital revenue from $400M to $1.25B while joining fragmented CRM, marketing automation, customer, call-center, and operational data. Through RevHub, my partner Harsh and I provide commercial architecture, analytics, forecasting, and implementation capacity.

For Interplay, a useful first step could be a multi-audience growth map across employers, education partners, workforce organizations, and skilled-trades learners, with shared definitions for buying triggers, campaign sources, qualification, pipeline, adoption, renewal, and expansion.

Would you be open to a 20-minute conversation to compare approaches? Our framework is here: https://www.aboutchad.com/approach

Best,
Chad Parker$$, $$Hi Emma,

One practical deliverable would be a marketing-to-revenue measurement architecture connecting segment, message, source, campaign, CRM stage, opportunity quality, product engagement, retention, and expansion. It would help the team distinguish activity that creates durable customer value from activity that only produces surface-level volume.

Would a brief discussion next week be worthwhile?$$, $$Hi Emma,

Closing the loop. I believe Interplay's RevOps build creates a strong opportunity for Marketing to establish shared lifecycle and value definitions before the systems and dashboards become fixed.

Is there a 20-minute window worth holding, or is another leader closer to that work?$$, $$Assumed from the first-initial-plus-last-name pattern demonstrated by the verified public address ddonovan@interplaylearning.com. This email may not work and should be verified before sending.$$);

insert into public.project_contacts (company, company_id, contact_name, title, linkedin_url, email, priority, target_type_raw, outreach_angle, value_hypothesis, stakeholder_selection_reason, linkedin_connect_message, intro_message, follow_up_message, email_subject, email_intro_message, email_follow_up_1, email_follow_up_2, email_assumption_notice) values ($$Interplay Learning$$, '0e7f66fc-334c-477d-814c-8b4219ee5777', $$Heather Granato$$, $$Senior Vice President, Customer Success & Services$$, $$https://www.linkedin.com/in/heatherhgranato$$, $$hgranato@interplaylearning.com$$, $$A/B$$, $$Target 3$$, $$The new RevOps function will govern the full revenue lifecycle and build shared reporting across Sales, Marketing, Customer Success, and Finance. Heather leads Customer Success and Services, making her the senior owner closest to onboarding, adoption, customer health, retention, expansion, and service-delivery signals.$$, $$Chad can help Heather connect implementation, learner and administrator engagement, support and service signals, customer health, renewal risk, expansion potential, and forecasting into one lifecycle model that makes the new RevOps infrastructure useful to customer-facing teams.$$, $$SVP-level customer lifecycle owner above the Senior Director signal. Heather Granato leads Customer Success & Services and is directly affected by the role's ownership of full-lifecycle analytics, customer-health reporting, retention, expansion, and cross-functional operating cadence.$$, $$Hi Heather, Interplay's current RevOps build caught my attention. Your leadership across Customer Success and Services makes the need to connect onboarding, engagement, customer health, retention, and expansion especially relevant. I would value connecting.$$, $$Thanks for connecting, Heather. Based on your work as Senior Vice President, Customer Success & Services at Interplay Learning, I wanted to be direct about why I reached out. RevHub helps organizations connect customer and market intelligence, segmentation, pricing, marketing, sales execution, operations, forecasting and executive reporting into one commercial growth system. For Interplay Learning, the opportunity I see is: Chad can help Heather connect implementation, learner and administrator engagement, support and service signals, customer health, renewal risk, expansion potential, and forecasting into one lifecycle model that makes the new RevOps infrastructure useful to customer-facing teams. Would you be open to a quick 15-minute call to discuss what we do and whether it could support your priorities?$$, $$Hi Heather, following up in case my earlier note got buried. I outlined the commercial growth approach behind RevHub here: https://aboutchad.com/approach. Based on your role at Interplay Learning, I believe there may be a relevant opportunity around the new revops function will govern the full revenue lifecycle and build shared reporting across sales, marketing, customer success, and finance. heather leads customer success and services, making her the senior owner closest to onboarding, adoption, customer health, retention, expansion, and service-delivery signals. Would you be open to a quick 15-minute call to discuss the approach and whether it could be useful to your team?$$, $$Connecting Interplay adoption, retention, and expansion$$, $$Hi Heather,

Interplay's search for its first dedicated Senior Director of Revenue Operations caught my attention because the role will only create value if the customer lifecycle is represented as clearly as pipeline and bookings. Your Customer Success and Services mandate sits at the center of implementation, adoption, learner and administrator engagement, service demand, renewal, and expansion.

I have spent more than 15 years building commercial operating systems that connect CRM, customer data, call-center and service signals, forecasting, analytics, and executive reporting. Through RevHub, my partner Harsh and I bring both commercial architecture and deeper analytical capacity.

For Interplay, a practical first step could define the customer-value model behind the RevOps build: implementation milestones, engagement signals, customer-health rules, renewal risk, expansion potential, ownership, and the forecast implications of each stage.

Would you be open to a 20-minute conversation to compare approaches? Our framework is here: https://www.aboutchad.com/approach

Best,
Chad Parker$$, $$Hi Heather,

One concrete deliverable could be a customer lifecycle and health architecture connecting implementation, usage, service demand, stakeholder adoption, renewal, expansion, and forecast confidence. It would help Customer Success, Revenue, and Finance prioritize accounts using shared evidence.

Would a brief conversation next week be useful?$$, $$Hi Heather,

Closing the loop. The opportunity I see is ensuring Interplay's new revenue operating model captures customer value and risk, not only pre-sale activity.

Is there a 20-minute window worth holding, or is another executive closer to the initial customer-lifecycle design?$$, $$Assumed from the first-initial-plus-last-name pattern demonstrated by the verified public address ddonovan@interplaylearning.com. This email may not work and should be verified before sending.$$);

